{:mulog/duration 10973667, :mulog/namespace "ai.obney.grain.todo-processor-v2.core", :mulog/outcome :ok, :mulog/parent-trace nil, :event {:tick-id #uuid "df4bf3e3-32e9-4394-a65f-5ddbcfc013bd", :event/tags #{[:sheet #uuid "9077987c-1f87-41e8-af6c-ee2b9b1ff3c4"] [:tick #uuid "df4bf3e3-32e9-4394-a65f-5ddbcfc013bd"] [:node #uuid "ac8e30ac-507a-46b2-a5e5-8050f07e36d7"]}, :event/timestamp #time/offset-date-time "2026-05-19T21:03:14.584338-05:00", :inputs {:document-survey "### Structure Analysis of microFIT Contracts (v2.0 vs v3.1.1)\n\n#### 1. Parts and Sections Present in Each\n**Common Structure (Present in both v2.0 and v3.1.1):**\n*   **Part 1 – Contract Details**\n    *   Section A: Contract and Supplier Information\n    *   Section B: Facility Characteristics\n    *   Section C: Documents Included; Defined Terms\n*   **Part 2 – General Terms and Conditions**\n    *   1. Term\n    *   2. Pricing Provisions and Payment\n    *   3. Environmental Attributes\n    *   4. Representations and Warranties\n    *   5. Covenants\n    *   6. General Conditions\n    *   7. Notices\n    *   8. Termination\n    *   9. Amendment\n    *   10. Assignment\n    *   11. Governing Law\n    *   12. Dispute Resolution\n    *   13. LDC Failure to Pay\n    *   14. Suppliers Composed of Multiple Entities\n*   **Appendices:**\n    *   Appendix A: Definitions\n    *   Appendix B: Calculation of Indexed Contract Price\n    *   Appendix C: Solar Photovoltaic Schedule\n    *   Appendix D-1: Directly Connected Facility Schedule\n    *   Appendix D-2: Indirectly Connected Facility Schedule\n    *   Appendix E: LDC Supplier Schedule\n\n#### 2. Unique and Shared Appendices/Sections\n| Component | Shared/Unique | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Part 1 & 2 Headers/Sections** | Shared | The high-level numbering and section titles (1 through 14) are identical. |\n| **Appendix C (Domestic Content)** | **Unique to v2.0** | Version 2.0 includes extensive technical grids and requirements for Domestic Content (Silicon, Inverters, etc.). Version 3.1.1 removes these grids and only retains basic definitions. |\n| **Domestic Content Level (4.4.4)** | **Unique to v2.0** | Explicit representation/warranty of Domestic Content level exists in v2.0; omitted in v3.1.1. |\n| **Prescribed Form Requirements** | **Unique to v3.1.1** | V3.1.1 introduces \"Prescribed Form\" requirements for changes to contact info (7.2), termination (8.1), and amendments (9). |\n| **\"Website\" Definition** | **Unique to v3.1.1** | Added to Appendix A in v3.1.1 to facilitate the use of online forms. |\n| **Emergency Disclosure (6.5)** | **Unique to v3.1.1** | V3.1.1 adds a provision allowing disclosure to agencies for public health, safety, and emergency response. |\n\n#### 3. Summary of Variations\n*   **Regulatory Focus:** v2.0 is focused heavily on the **Domestic Content Power Project** requirements (specifically for Solar PV), including detailed calculation tables. v3.1.1 streamlines the contract by removing these technical grids.\n*   **Administrative Modernization:** v3.1.1 introduces the concept of **\"Prescribed Forms\"** and refers to the OPA website for the latest versions of documents, whereas v2.0 is more static in its procedural descriptions.\n*   **Safety/Disclosure:** v3.1.1 expands the \"Disclosure of Information\" section to include emergency services and public health officials.", :section-diffs "The Following is a comprehensive comparison of microFIT Contract Version 2.0 and Version 3.1.1.\n\n### 1. Major Sections Remaining Identical\nThe following sections and appendices remain substantively identical in text and structure, with only minor formatting or capitalization updates:\n*   **Part 2, Section 1 (Term)**\n*   **Part 2, Section 2 (Pricing Provisions and Payment)**\n*   **Part 2, Section 3 (Environmental Attributes)**\n*   **Part 2, Section 11 (Governing Law)**\n*   **Part 2, Section 13 (LDC Failure to Pay)**\n*   **Part 2, Section 14 (Suppliers Composed of Multiple Entities)**\n*   **Appendix B (Calculation of Indexed Contract Price)**\n*   **Appendix D-1 (Directly Connected Facility Schedule)**\n*   **Appendix D-2 (Indirectly Connected Facility Schedule)**\n*   **Appendix E (LDC Supplier Schedule)**\n\n### 2. Specific Additions, Removals, and Modifications\n\n#### Part 1: Contract Details & Facility Characteristics\n*   **Section B(5)(iv) Removal:** The requirement to state the \"Minimum Required Domestic Content Level\" (60% in v2.0) has been removed in v3.1.1.\n*   **Section B(8) Modification:** v2.0 included a check-box for wind capacity (3 kW or less vs greater than 3 kW). In v3.1.1, these checkboxes remain but the formatting suggests a reduction in specific fuel-related sub-questions in Part 1 compared to the more expansive v2.0 list.\n\n#### Part 2: General Terms and Conditions\n*   **Section 4.4.4 Removal:** The specific representation that a Solar PV facility must have a \"Domestic Content Level greater than or equal to the Minimum Required Domestic Content Level\" has been removed.\n*   **Section 5.1.2 Modification:** Added the requirement that notification of changes must be in the **\"Prescribed Form.\"**\n*   **Section 6.5 (Collection/Disclosure of Information) Addition:** v3.1.1 adds a new provision under \"Disclosure of Information\" allowing OPA to disclose facility details to government ministries/agencies for \"promoting public health and safety\" and \"emergency services personnel.\"\n*   **Section 6.5 Modification:** Removed the specific reference to sharing information for \"compliance with Minimum Required Domestic Content Level.\"\n*   **Section 7.2 (Notice) Modification:** Added that changes to contact information must be in the **\"Prescribed Form.\"**\n*   **Section 8.1 (Termination) Modification:** Added that notice of termination by the Supplier must be in the **\"Prescribed Form.\"**\n*   **Section 9 (Amendment) Modification:** Added that amendments must be reduced to writing in the **\"Prescribed Form.\"**\n\n#### Appendix A: Definitions\n*   **\"Domestic Content Level\" Removal:** Definition deleted in v3.1.1.\n*   **\"Minimum Required Domestic Content Level\" Removal:** Definition deleted in v3.1.1.\n*   **\"Prescribed Form\" Addition:** New definition added in v3.1.1 meaning \"the latest version of the corresponding form appearing on the Website.\"\n*   **\"Website\" Addition:** New definition added in v3.1.1 providing the URL `http://microfit.powerauthority.on.ca`.\n*   **\"Project Eligibility Requirements\" (Item c) Modification:** v3.1.1 adds the qualifier \"unless otherwise consented to by the OPA in writing in its sole and absolute discretion\" regarding facilities that have previously reached commercial operation.\n\n#### Appendix C: Solar Photovoltaic Schedule\n*   **Definition of \"Existing Building\" Modification:** v3.1.1 adds part (b), allowing the OPA \"in its sole and absolute discretion\" to issue written confirmation deeming a building as an \"Existing Building.\"\n*   **Domestic Content Requirements (Sections 1.1 through 1.7) Removal:** All text regarding Domestic Content calculations, Designated Activities, and the calculation of Qualifying Percentages has been removed.\n*   **Domestic Content Grids (Tables 1, 2, and 3) Removal:** The entirety of the \"Domestic Content Grid\" tables (Crystalline Silicon, Thin-Film, and Concentrated PV) found on pages 17-19 of v2.0 has been removed in v3.1.1.", :major-changes "Based on the provided section-diffs and contract texts, the major changes are as follows:\n\n1.  **Elimination of Domestic Content Requirements**: \n    - The most significant change is the complete removal of all \"Domestic Content\" mandates. In v2.0, Solar PV projects were required to maintain a 60% domestic content level, supported by detailed grids and reporting requirements (Appendix C). In v3.1.1, these sections, definitions, and representations (Section 4.4.4) have been entirely deleted.\n\n2.  **Implementation of \"Prescribed Forms\" for Contract Actions**:\n    - v3.1.1 introduces a requirement that key administrative actions—including change of contact info (Section 7.2), supplier termination (Section 8.1), and contract amendments (Section 9)—must now be executed using a \"Prescribed Form\" found on the OPA website. v2.0 allowed for more general written notice.\n\n3.  **Expanded Information Disclosure for Public Safety**:\n    - Section 6.5 has been updated to grant the OPA authority to disclose facility and supplier information to government agencies and emergency services for \"public health and safety\" and \"emergency response\" planning.\n\n4.  **Discretionary Eligibility and Building Classification**:\n    - Appendix A (\"Project Eligibility Requirements\") and Appendix C (\"Existing Building\") were modified to grant the OPA \"sole and absolute discretion\" to grant exceptions for previously contracted facilities or to deem certain structures as \"Existing Buildings\" for Rooftop Facility classification.", :impact-analysis "For each major change, the shift in risk and benefit is analyzed below:\n\n1.  **Elimination of Domestic Content Requirements**:\n    - **Shift**: Significant benefit to the **Supplier**.\n    - **Analysis**: This removes a substantial compliance burden and legal risk. Under v2.0, failing to meet the 60% threshold was a breach of warranty. Suppliers now have greater flexibility in sourcing equipment (e.g., inverters, modules) from global markets rather than being restricted to Ontario-based manufacturers.\n\n2.  **Requirement of \"Prescribed Forms\"**:\n    - **Shift**: Benefit to the **OPA**.\n    - **Analysis**: This streamlines OPA administration but increases the procedural burden on the **Supplier**. If a supplier provides notice of termination or change of address in a standard letter rather than the OPA’s specific \"Prescribed Form,\" the OPA could technically deem the notice invalid.\n\n3.  **Expanded Information Disclosure**:\n    - **Shift**: Benefit to the **OPA/Public Authority**; Risk to the **Supplier**.\n    - **Analysis**: This broadens the scope of how the Supplier's data is used. While it serves a public safety purpose, it slightly reduces the Supplier's privacy protections regarding facility data and location.\n\n4.  **Discretionary Eligibility and Building Classification**:\n    - **Shift**: Benefit to the **OPA**.\n    - **Analysis**: By adding \"sole and absolute discretion\" language, the OPA gains the power to act as the final arbiter on eligibility disputes without being strictly bound by the definitions. While this allows for flexibility that *could* benefit a Supplier (e.g., getting a project approved that otherwise wouldn't fit), the legal weight gives the OPA total control over the interpretation, making it harder for Suppliers to challenge OPA decisions in court or arbitration."}, :node-id #uuid "ac8e30ac-507a-46b2-a5e5-8050f07e36d7", :event/type :sheet/node-execution-started, :sheet-id #uuid "9077987c-1f87-41e8-af6c-ee2b9b1ff3c4", :event/id #uuid "019e431f-7118-700e-bba6-fb3aa7427897"}, :mulog/root-trace #mulog/flake "5A3YhcCpoomhGeX5cHO01kBDEf8CpOz5", :mulog/timestamp 1779242594584, :metric/name "TodoProcessed", :mulog/trace-id #mulog/flake "5A3YhcCpoomhGeX5cHO01kBDEf8CpOz5", :mulog/event-name :ai.obney.grain.todo-processor-v2.core/processing-event, :metric/resolution :high}
